\section{Inference}{

CAMS can be fitted using Bayesian or frequentist inference. These are not
two different scientific models. The approaches are \strong{bayesmeta} (DIRECT,
the default) and \strong{metafor} (REML or ML). See \code{vignette("engine-comparison")}.

Computational knobs (quadrature controls, optimizer settings) go in
\code{engine_args}, not as model arguments.

\code{\link[=swada]{swada()}}, \code{\link[=da]{da()}}, and \code{\link[=ad]{ad()}} are \strong{not} alternative CAMS models. SWADA
does not use the CAMS information-fraction (IF) construction; IF
quantities and IF-based plots are not available for SWADA objects.
Comparison helpers may combine multiple user-supplied fits only when
those fits are passed explicitly.
}

\section{Related analyses}{

CAMS is the \strong{statistical model}. \code{\link[=da]{da()}} (Difference of Averages) and
\code{\link[=ad]{ad()}} (Average Difference) are \strong{different constructions} (Panaro,
Röver & Friede 2026) for a treatment-by-subgroup interaction from
subgroup-level aggregate data. They can use different study weights and
need not agree. \code{\link[=swada]{swada()}} applies the SWADA \emph{weighting principle} so
those analyses share one contribution vector; under the default
Interaction RE-weights, the DA interaction equals AD. \code{\link[=conventional]{conventional()}}
is a compatibility bundle of DA and AD. None of these is the 2002 van
Houwelingen joint bivariate mixed model.
}

\section{Method}{

\code{method} is the inferential method (\code{"bayes"}, \code{"REML"} / \code{"ML"}), not the
approach name (\code{bayesmeta} vs \code{metafor}). Prefer
\code{\link[=bayescam]{bayescam()}} for Bayesian CAMS and \code{cam(..., engine = "metafor")} for
frequentist CAMS. The fit stores \code{fit$inference}, \code{fit$engine}, \code{fit$method},
and \code{fit$algorithm} (\code{"quadrature"} or \code{"optimization"}).
}

\section{Priors}{

Bayesian CAMS uses one CAMS-level prior specification (see \code{\link[=cam_prior]{cam_prior()}}),
stored on \code{fit$priors}. Defaults are:
\describe{
\item{fixed effects}{\eqn{\mathrm{Normal}(0, 10)} on intercept, IF slope
\eqn{\beta}, and subgroup offset \eqn{\gamma}}
\item{\code{tau.prior}}{\eqn{\mathrm{Normal}^+(0, 0.5)} on overall
heterogeneity \eqn{\tau_m}}
\item{\code{tau.interaction.prior}}{\eqn{\mathrm{Normal}^+(0, 0.1)} on
interaction heterogeneity \eqn{\tau_g}; if \code{NULL} and the user supplied
\code{tau.prior}, the overall prior is inherited}
}
Character shorthands \code{"uniform"} and \code{"sqrt"} are the \strong{improper} bayesmeta
densities \eqn{p(\tau)\propto 1} and \eqn{p(\tau)\propto \tau^{-1/2}}
(\eqn{\tau \ge 0}). They are \strong{not} \eqn{U(0, U)}.
\code{engine = "metafor"} ignores Bayesian priors (warning if the user supplied
them).

\strong{Fixed-effect caveat:} bayesmeta/bmr place independent \eqn{N(0, 10)}
on \eqn{(\alpha,\theta)} with \eqn{\theta = \beta + \gamma} and on
\eqn{\gamma}. Stored \code{b_ifrac} is \eqn{\beta=\theta-\gamma}.
}

